course: dictionary
lesson: 4086
title: Gibbon: a word for the trees
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gibbon is a small ape with very long arms and no tail. Look for clues around the word: «Gibbons are small apes that sing loud duets» tells you a gibbon is an ape, and that gibbons make loud songs.
explain: The lessons also tell you how gibbons move. «Swinging arm over arm is called brachiation» means the swinging has a name, and it is the way gibbons travel through the trees. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
explain: Some words are needed to explain a fact about a gibbon. «Like all apes, orangutans, gibbons and bonobos have no tails» shows that gibbons are part of a family of apes that share this feature.
fact: A gibbon is a small ape with very long arms and no tail.
fact: Gibbons live in the rainforests of Southeast Asia.
fact: «Brachiation» means swinging arm over arm from branch to branch.
fact: A «duet» is a song sung by two singers together, like a male and female gibbon.
fact: Many gibbons are endangered because their forests are cut down.
exercise: Based on the meaning of gibbon, what is a gibbon? | A small fish that swims in rivers | A kind of tree with long, thin leaves | *A small ape with very long arms and no tail | A large bird that flies over the rainforest
exercise: «Swinging arm over arm is called brachiation.» What does brachiation mean? | Eating fruit from the ground | *Swinging arm over arm through the trees | Digging holes in the sand | Sleeping high up in a nest
exercise: According to «Like all apes, orangutans, gibbons and bonobos have no tails», what is true of gibbons? | *They have no tail | They use their tail to swing from branch to branch | They have a long, curly tail | They have a tail only when they are babies
test: In «Gibbons are small apes that sing loud duets», what does duets suggest? | *Songs sung by two gibbons together | A fight between two animals | A kind of dance on the forest floor | A quiet whisper from one gibbon alone
test: Why are many gibbons endangered? | *Because their forests are cut down | Because they have too many tails | Because they live in deserts | Because they are too fast to catch
exam: Read: «In 2017 the Skywalker hoolock gibbon was named as a new species, and fewer than about two hundred are thought to live in the wild.» What does this suggest? | *Very few of this gibbon are left in the wild | Gibbons are found in every forest in the world | Exactly two hundred gibbons live in the wild | Scientists have never studied gibbons
